1972 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Recommendations\n\nRedesign modifier interaction\072 Replace the underline\055based tap pattern with a more visually prominent control \050toggle switches\054 stepper controls\054 or clearly labeled buttons\051\056 Provide immediate visual feedback when a modifier is selected\056 Consider progressive disclosure to reduce cognitive overload rather than displaying all modifier options simultaneously\056\n\nRestructure information hierarchy\072 Reduce the screen real estate given to recommendations on the main ordering screen\056 Make the menu navigation more prominent\056 Consider moving recommendations below the menu or into a dedicated tab so they don\047t block the primary ordering path\056\n\nSurface product information\072 Add more prominent ingredient lists and allergen flags to each menu item at the point of selection\056 Replace ambiguous flavor adjectives with concrete descriptions\056 This addresses a usability gap and a potential business liability\056\n\nFix the special instructions field\072 Display the character count\056 Increase the character limit\054 especially given that users are relying on this field as a modifier workaround\056 Ensure the keyboard does not obscure the text input area on mobile\056\n\nImprove onboarding\072 The \042Let\047s Go\042 screen was skipped or misunderstood by several participants\056 Consider auto\055detecting the nearest store location and simplifying the welcome flow to reduce initial orientation time and potential conversion loss\056)
     /K [ 4 ]
     /P 1973 0 R
     /Pg 1916 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

1973 0 obj
  << /K [ 1972 0 R ]
     /P 1978 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 1916 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

1974 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Successes\072\n\nCart and checkout was the most consistently successful task\056 Participants found removing items\054 changing quantities\054 and completing checkout to be straightforward across both platforms\056 \n\nLocation selection also performed well\054 with most participants finding their store without difficulty\056 The drink category structure \050coffee\057tea separation\051 made sense to participants once the found it\054 and was described positively\056 \n\nNotably\054 younger participants tended to navigate the interface with less friction\054 while older or less tech\055familiar participants struggled more with the app\047s non\055standard interaction patterns\056 This suggests the interface relies on conventions that are not yet widely understood across user demographics\056)
     /K [ 5 ]
     /P 1975 0 R
     /Pg 1916 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

